1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
There's nothing medical science can do for you.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,880
The second thing sounds like,

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:05,720
you know, like if they say it,

4
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:08,560
they sound like they don't really love you, right?

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,200
Although you might think,

6
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,900
well maybe they love me more than other people.

7
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:15,060
Maybe they love me enough to value my time.

8
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,100
Maybe they love me enough to want to protect me

9
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,640
against getting sick in a hospital,

10
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:24,340
which is a very common occurrence, right?

11
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:25,840
So there's a lot of research estimating

12
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:29,880
how many people die of diseases they catch in a hospital.

13
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:32,320
The numbers are always really large.

14
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:34,860
It certainly should be enough to always tip the scales

15
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:36,360
against going if you're unsure.

16
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:40,080
Well, maybe I should go, maybe I shouldn't.

17
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:45,080
But 10,000 people a year die because they went

18
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:46,680
and they wouldn't have died otherwise

19
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:48,000
because they caught a disease

20
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,320
from a sick person in a hospital,

21
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,000
which is where they are.

22
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,160
So worth thinking about.

23
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:54,840
Anyway, you can go and read Robin's paper

24
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:55,800
for more of the argument.

25
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:59,480
But over time, while I wouldn't say that I'm convinced,

26
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,720
I'm definitely more convinced than I was at first

27
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:05,040
and noticed a lot of facts that seems to fit.

28
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:08,200
And then I'll just mention one small but fun one.

29
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:09,280
Funerals.

30
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:10,560
So think about embalming.

31
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:13,280
This is a medical procedure we apply to the dead.

32
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,960
It's clearly not for the benefit

33
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,920
of the person that we're embalming, right?

34
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:21,120
Unless you believe in ancient Egyptian religion

35
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:22,520
and you think you can't go to the afterlife

36
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,800
unless you're unembalmed or something,

37
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:25,400
if you're unembalmed.

38
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,360
So why do we go and spend all this money on funerals?

39
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:31,240
It's a medical procedure for the dead.

40
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:33,360
So of course, you may say, well, we do it to go and show

41
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,720
that we cared about the person for closure.

42
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,160
But then you realize, well, but what happens

43
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:41,600
if you go and cheap out on a funeral?

44
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:45,120
And why do people not cheap out on funerals nearly as much

45
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:46,600
as they might like?

46
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:50,200
Like I know my dad, my dad's a cheap guy

47
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,760
and his dad before him was also very cheap

48
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:54,920
and didn't inspire a lot of affection.

49
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:58,720
But, and my dad actually very soon afterwards said,

50
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:00,160
well, people wouldn't talk.

51
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:03,160
I would just put them in a pine box, but if people talk,

52
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:05,840
so I got to go and pretend to be a loving son.

53
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:08,680
He didn't actually put it that way, but you've got,

54
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:11,840
you've got to go and get this $5,000 coffin.

55
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:13,920
People were expecting it, right?

56
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:17,040
So there, it seemed very much like he felt

57
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:18,520
like he had to signal devotion.

58
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:21,160
If he went and did what was in his heart,

59
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,040
people would have talked about what a terrible son he was.

60
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:26,240
Right, and it seems plausible.

61
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:27,600
This is common in funerals.

62
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:31,280
The people go and spend way more than they actually want

63
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:34,480
to spend in order to go and express their love.

64
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:37,720
So a lot of what you're doing is feeling this pressure.

65
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:39,560
I have to go and put a lot of money into this

66
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:42,480
or else I will be considered unloving and a bad son,

67
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:45,200
bad husband, bad whatever.

68
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:48,240
All right, so you judge for yourself.

69
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,440
But you know, I mean, I've always creeped out at funerals

70
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:54,920
by how everyone who dies apparently was a wonderful person.

71
00:02:54,920 --> 00:02:58,280
Right, so it's like, he wasn't.

72
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:01,280
Right, and you say, well, what are we supposed to say?

73
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:02,720
How about the truth?

74
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:04,440
Like what's, like if we can,

75
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:06,760
if we only have five minutes of good truthing to say,

76
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:09,260
let's make it five minutes and stop.

77
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:10,760
That's how I feel.

78
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:12,720
And to me, there's just something so Orwellian

79
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:15,280
about making up a bunch of lies about a person.

80
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:18,640
You know, that's, but anyway, that's also me.

81
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:22,360
Probably another way in which I'm not a normal human being.

82
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:23,200
Right, that's.

83
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:25,160
That is a moving illustration right there.

84
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:26,000
Yeah.

85
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:26,960
Yeah.

86
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:29,640
Yes, well, yes, I once tweeted,

87
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:31,920
well, you can reverse and just say,

88
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:34,360
don't do, never do anything that would make someone

89
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:36,000
bend the truth at your funeral.

90
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,800
Just live a life so admirable that no one has to lie.

91
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:42,440
No one has to sit around making up stuff.

92
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:44,000
But most people don't do that.

93
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,480
All right, now, big question.

94
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,040
So if singling is a deadweight cost,

95
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:51,240
could government action make matters more efficient?

96
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:54,360
And the answer is yes, government could tax the signal.

97
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:58,820
Now, if there is some combination of useful good

98
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:01,720
and signal going on, then maybe there's a problem.

99
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,620
Of course, the same thing is true for any tax

100
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:06,240
on any negative externality.

101
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:09,320
You might say, well, what if this chemical

102
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:12,240
is dangerous at high levels, but it's helpful at low levels,

103
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:14,480
or it's good for some things, but bad for others?

104
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:15,900
And of course, the normal thing is you just try

105
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:18,120
to strike an average, right?

106
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:20,300
So, you know, like carbon dioxide, it's bad for warming,

107
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:21,920
but it's good for plants.

108
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:25,120
So put it all together, good for plant growth.

109
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:28,220
Okay, but anyway, the basic economics is you tax the signal

110
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:29,960
and then everyone could, for example,

111
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:31,600
get half as much education

112
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:33,360
and still get the same job offers.

113
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:37,460
So this is, again, this is basic economics.

114
00:04:37,460 --> 00:04:40,040
With singling, there's a negative externality.

115
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:42,440
The negative externality is caused by the fact

116
00:04:42,440 --> 00:04:45,120
that if you unilaterally signal more,

117
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:48,480
you improve your status and get more money

118
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:51,080
without improving the productivity of mankind,

119
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:53,480
and therefore, it is not only redistributive,

120
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:57,440
it is redistribution with a negative sum,

121
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:59,040
and with the tax, you would reduce

122
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:00,880
this negative sum behavior, which is, of course,

123
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:03,900
the whole point of Bergovian taxation, right?

124
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,160
All right, right, and again, so when I mentioned,

125
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:10,440
so in like the clearest cases,

126
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:12,400
it's actually this credential inflation.

127
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:15,800
So as the average number of years of education has gone up,

128
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:18,520
the average number of years of education

129
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:20,440
you need to get a job has gone up, too.

130
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:23,760
So since World War II, roughly, for any given job,

131
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:25,360
you need about four more years of education

132
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:26,760
than you used to have.

133
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:28,840
So basically, right now, jobs that used to go

134
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,140
to high school dropouts go to high school graduates.

135
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:32,520
Jobs that used to go to high school graduates

136
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:33,600
now go to college graduates.

137
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:34,880
Jobs that used to go to college graduates

138
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:37,000
go to people with graduate degrees.

139
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,960
Where's the negative externality here?

140
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:41,180
Well, as education levels have risen,

141
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:43,120
naturally, the market equilibrium has increased

142
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:45,120
the amount of education you're expected to have,

143
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:48,240
and what would be the point of the tax?

144
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:51,000
Rollback, just like the Reagan doctrine.

145
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:52,720
We're gonna roll back communism.

146
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,360
We're gonna go and roll back education.

147
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:55,840
We're gonna go back to a world

148
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:58,000
where waiters don't have college degrees.

149
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,660
We're gonna go back to a world where high school dropouts

150
00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:01,840
can get all kinds of jobs

151
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:04,600
because they're a third of the population.

152
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:06,840
And you can't be so picky in the world

153
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:09,800
where high school dropouts are a third of the population,

154
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:11,440
but also, it's just not as scary

155
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:12,600
to hire a high school dropout

156
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:13,720
when they're a third of the population.

157
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:15,360
They're not that bad.

158
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,000
Whereas in the modern world,

159
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:19,760
you are very concerned

160
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:23,080
because who doesn't finish high school in our day and age?

161
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:25,680
Those people who have a bunch of different problems.

162
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:30,880
Really not very smart, really lazy, or really defiant.

163
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:32,320
You know, think about the smart person

164
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:34,040
who hasn't finished high school.

165
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:36,960
Anyone know a really smart high school dropout?

166
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:40,800
How many problems do they have?

167
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:43,440
Right, they are not good workers on average,

168
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:45,920
but of course, implicitly signaling model

169
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:49,240
is that there could be some people

170
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:52,600
who are awesome even though they haven't sent the signal.

171
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:54,400
But again, the problem is that you're judged

172
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:55,520
based upon average levels,

173
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:57,720
so by forcing the average level down,

174
00:06:57,720 --> 00:07:00,920
you are changing the way that everyone is perceived.

175
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,560
So just imagine a world where you could be a professor

176
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:07,680
when you, after a year or two of a master's degree.

177
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:10,600
Like back in the 30s, there's people who would,

178
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:11,680
they go and they start a master's

179
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:12,920
and then they get hired as a professor.

180
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:14,360
They don't even finish their master's,

181
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:15,760
never mind the PhD.

182
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:19,160
Now of course, almost all professors are the PhDs.

183
00:07:19,160 --> 00:07:20,840
Now this is an area where,

184
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:23,000
like isn't government, doesn't government make this?

185
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:24,720
So there's probably some accreditation thing

186
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:27,000
where if you have too many non-PhD professors,

187
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:28,440
you may have a problem,

188
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:31,600
but most of it is, you know, it's a,

189
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:33,680
when there's tons of PhDs going around,

190
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:37,040
you naturally raise the educational credentials required

191
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:40,120
because like we're trying to get people in the top 5%.

192
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:43,840
So if there's 5% of the people have degrees this good,

193
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:46,560
then we'll go and make that the cutoff.

194
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,440
My dad actually told me that when,

195
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:50,720
that he actually saw credential inflation

196
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:52,440
right during his own career.

197
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:54,360
So in his 30 years of an engineer,

198
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:56,680
the first job that he got hired for

199
00:07:56,680 --> 00:07:59,000
is one where you only needed two years of college

200
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,520
and they hire you.

201
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:02,680
But 10 years later, for the very same job you needed

202
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:05,200
to actually have the engineering degree in hand,

203
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:07,840
then 10 years later, you needed the master's degree.

204
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:08,840
And when he's on his way out,

205
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:09,680
I think there was some talk,

206
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:12,400
maybe you need a PhD to go and do this job.

207
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:14,080
Right, and what's going on is just that

208
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:17,360
as you ramp up the prevalence of degrees,

209
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:18,920
you raise standards.

210
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:24,960
And as a result, we wind up having this rat race.

211
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:26,560
Right, people often say, well,

212
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:27,880
they're like, it's like socially unjust

213
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:29,840
to go and fight credential inflation.

214
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:32,160
Well, here, people are always thinking about

215
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:33,480
the person from a bad neighborhood

216
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,640
who worked their way up and got a PhD, right?

217
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:36,800
And like my best friend at Princeton

218
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:39,120
was a guy who grew up in a log cabin

219
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:42,080
that they built themselves in the Pocono Mountains.

220
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:44,200
Right, and like his mom burned all their furniture,

221
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:45,400
like terrible, terrible.

222
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:48,040
Well, he felt his childhood was great.

223
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:49,200
He had four brothers, they all had fun.

224
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,040
But anyway, almost anyone would say

225
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:53,280
he had an extremely disadvantaged childhood,

226
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:58,080
but nevertheless, he wound up getting a PhD from Princeton.

227
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:01,240
Right, and for him, of course, the system worked.

228
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:03,560
But there's a whole lot of other people in his shoes

229
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:07,320
for whom the system is way worse than it was in 1950.

230
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:09,280
Like who is worse off, you know,

231
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:11,760
like who has that background is worse off today

232
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:12,960
than they were in 1950?

233
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:18,400
How about people who dropped out of high school?

234
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:21,720
Back in 1950, if one of my friend's brothers

235
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:23,040
dropped out of high school,

236
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:25,360
then he's in the bottom third of the distribution.

237
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:26,200
That's not that bad.

238
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:29,840
He just goes and gets a regular job.

239
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:33,080
But today, if you have like that same person who drops out,

240
00:09:33,080 --> 00:09:36,680
who just doesn't wind up getting any of the credentials,

241
00:09:36,680 --> 00:09:39,720
right, is really in the bottom of the distribution.

242
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:42,440
Like very few jobs would even be considered.

243
00:09:42,440 --> 00:09:44,080
So that's the difference,

244
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:45,280
is that when people think about

245
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:47,320
the social justice of the system,

246
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:49,280
they're always focusing on the upside.

247
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:51,000
People from humble backgrounds

248
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,920
who managed to rise through education,

249
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:55,320
they're never thinking about people of humble backgrounds

250
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:56,840
who are judged much more harshly

251
00:09:56,840 --> 00:09:57,920
than they would have been in the past

252
00:09:57,920 --> 00:10:00,560
because average education levels are so much higher.

253
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:02,400
See, I think we are out of time.

254
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:03,240
Yes, we are actually.

255
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:08,240
So I'm happy to take questions beginning next class.


